Remember the Core Principles of Weather Reporting
• Immediacy and Authority: Broadcast all official National Weather Service (NWS) watches, warnings, and storm bulletins promptly. Clearly identify the issuing authority and valid time windows.
• Accuracy and Uncertainty: Avoid sensational or alarmist headlines. Communicate forecast confidence, range of uncertainty, and specific computer models or data sources being used.
• Public Safety Priority: Place human life and protective action above entertainment, visual gimmicks, or commercial pressure. Distinguish clearly between short-range detailed forecasts (under 7 days) and extended outlooks (8 days and beyond, which have lower skill levels).
• Collaboration with Agencies: Work closely with the NWS, emergency management personnel, and local safety networks.
• Journalistic Rigor: Treat weather as a serious editorial beat rather than just casual filler. Verify user-generated content, social media videos, and field reports before broadcasting them.

